1904 0 obj
<<
  /Type /StructElem
  /S /Figure
  /P 1903 0 R
  /Alt (This image sequence illustrates the detection of land cover change in Vienna, Austria, using Root Mean Square Deviation (RMSD) normalized by land cover class. It features three false-colour composite satellite imagery panels showing the same geographic area, with white lines outlining individual parcels. The **left panel** displays the initial state of the area, where vegetation appears in red. Key land cover types are indicated by yellow CLC+ Backbone codes: '11100' (Continuous urban fabric) in the top-middle, '11210' (Discontinuous urban fabric) below it, '23000' (Pastures) in the large central parcel, and two '11230' (Construction sites) in the lower section. The **middle panel** shows a later state of the same area. A significant change is visible in the central parcel previously classified as '23000' (Pastures), where the red vegetation has been replaced by bright white/cyan tones, indicating new construction or bare ground. The **right panel** is a heatmap of the normalized RMSD (RMSD_norm), which highlights pixels where observed change is significantly greater than what is typical for that land cover type. The colour gradient ranges from dark blue (lowest change) through light blue, green, and yellow, to dark red (highest change). The central parcel, where substantial land cover conversion occurred, shows a high concentration of yellow, orange, and dark red pixels, indicating a strong signal of significant change. Minor change signals (yellow pixels) are also visible in other parcels.)
  /A [<<
    /O /Layout
    /Placement /Block
  >>]
  /K [38]
  /Pg 2574 0 R
>>
endobj

